WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.710
Nicole: An Incident post-mortem takes
place after the event to help teams

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:06.720
better prepare for future incidents.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:11.450
Abraham: These are also known as
‘post incident reviews’ or ‘root cause

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:16.079
analysis’ and doing this can help create
a culture of continuous improvement.

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:19.520
Nicole: It shouldn’t specifically
apportion blame but should be

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.509
about understanding why and how
the incident took place and how

00:00:23.509 --> 00:00:25.230
future attacks can be prevented.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.889
All parties should be able to
speak more freely when blame

00:00:28.889 --> 00:00:30.400
is taken out of the equation.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.220
Abraham: At the time of the attack
the first response is to restore

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.100
service and mitigate the incident.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.440
But the post-mortem gives chance
to take the whole incident

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.339
apart and see what went wrong.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.400
The person in charge is the incident
post-mortem coordinator and will

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.339
be chosen by the person in charge
of responding to the incident.

00:00:49.759 --> 00:00:53.019
Nicole: The coordinator will then
involve and liaise with the other

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:56.810
members of the appropriate teams who were
involved in responding to the incident.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:01.390
Once they have accumulated the
relevant information, they’ll organise

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.509
a discussion and assemble a report.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:08.740
A post-mortem is usually a collaborative
process orchestrated by the coordinator.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.740
Abraham: Most organisations will
develop their own post-mortem

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.380
process, structure and documentation
according to their needs.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.280
But on the whole, they
often follow the same steps:

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.650
Nicole: The meeting- this will
involve all those who were

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.140
involved in the incident response.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:28.319
Any documentation such as
incident logs should be presented.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:33.199
There may also be more individual meetings
to gain more details with those involved

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.260
in mitigating the incident directly.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:40.619
It should also be made clear to all team
members, that the incident was blameless!

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.139
Abraham: The Post-mortem Report-this
presents all the information and

00:01:45.139 --> 00:01:48.849
conclusions gained from the meeting
and a record of the event that

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:50.360
can be referred to in the future.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:55.679
It should include incident details,
root cause analysis, the incident

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:59.920
timeline, any actions, and any
conclusions and steps to be taken.

00:02:00.679 --> 00:02:04.280
Nicole: Dissemination of the
report and findings- this should

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.250
be distributed to all involved and
to management and decision makers.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:12.400
It may also need to be made public
to reassure customers and clients.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.710
Abraham: Follow up actions-
If recommendations are made

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:17.929
these should be acted on.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:22.900
An incident post-mortem should be a valid
process and not just a tick box exercise.

00:02:23.529 --> 00:02:27.290
It should be clearly detailed enough to
draw conclusions as to what works and

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.540
what doesn’t and how this can be remedied.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.180
Nicole: Remember incidents are
a learning opportunity, a chance

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.019
to uncover vulnerabilities
and mitigate future incidents.

